+
+
+.. _hookimpl_arg_default:
+
+Hookimpl argument defaults
+^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
+A *hookimpl* can also declare a default value for an argument. The value the
+*hookimpl* receives is determined with the following precedence:
+
+1. the value passed by the hook call,
+2. the default value declared by the *hookspec*,
+3. the default value declared by the *hookimpl* itself.
+
+The *hookimpl* default is therefore only used when neither the caller nor the
+*hookspec* provides the argument, for example when a new *hookimpl* runs
+against an older *hookspec* which does not have the argument yet:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    @hookimpl
+    def myhook(config, args, new_arg="fallback"):
+        pass
+
+
+    # hookimpl gets new_arg="get this".
+    pm.hook.myhook(config=config, args=args, new_arg="get this")
+    # hookimpl gets the hookspec default if there is one, otherwise "fallback".
+    pm.hook.myhook(config=config, args=args)
+
+This applies to plain implementations as well as to wrappers.
+
+.. versionchanged:: 1.7
+    Values passed by the hook call and *hookspec* defaults now take precedence
+    over *hookimpl* defaults. Previously the *hookimpl* default was always used.
 
 
